  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n- Light rain possible tonight\n\nA negatively tilted mid level ridge will by driving to the east \nby an approaching upper level system moving through western \nCanada. This will bring some warm air advection into Michigan late\ntoday through Saturday. Given that warm air advection, there \ncould be enough moisture and lift to bring some light rain this \nevening. The warm air advection will send Max Temps between 10 to \n15 degrees above normal through the weekend and into Monday. While\nclear skies during the evening will lead to some crisp mornings, \nSaturday could see temps in the mid to upper 70s. If skies clear \nthere is a chance to reach 80. \n\n- Isaias could bring some showers Sunday\n\n The next chance for any appreciable rain will be from the\nremnants of Isaias Sunday. While its not to prophetical to say,\nthe best chance for rain on Sunday will be through the eastern\nhalf of the state. \n Models are fairly consistent, though it has trended eastward, \nand the track with NHC having it moving through KY as a depression\nSunday. Expect increase clouds with showers along and east of the\nUS 127 corridor. The ridge of high pressure to its west,\ncorrelated with deepening low over the west coast will keep Isaias\nfrom shifting to the west. \n\n The warm air will continue to dominate the area through Monday\nwith highs continuing in the 70s. \n\n- Pattern change with Cold front Monday into Tuesday\n\nA cold front is still on track to swing through the area late\nMonday into Tuesday. While QPF remains spotty, the flow will shift\nto the northwest and bring temperatures down closer to normal. So\nenjoy the warmth as Autumn awaits you through the latter half of \nthe upcoming week. That said, lows will only drop into the 40s\nthrough much of the region.\n The next system to keep an eye out for will be previously\nmentioned low over the west coast. That will trek across the\nstates through the week and is currently trending an arrival time\non Thursday. There is quite a bit of difference e on the \nposition of the lows and its orientation. given that confidence \nis low on when it will arrive and how much QPF will be associated\nwith it when it swings through Michigan. The GEFS does have \nanomalous PWATS upwards of 1.25 through the lower peninsula \nThursday. So there is the potential for widespread rain Thursday.",
